# ── Chapter 12 fidelity pins: "Changing the Rules" ───────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/12-changing-the-rules.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# The "three ways this is thinner" section is held two ways: the self-declared
# target (Amend_Sneak) is a DECLARED defect below — repairing it exits 3 and
# names the fix — and "become() feeds nothing" is an absence no pin can hold,
# so it is a :require precondition with a positive control beside it, the
# chapter-8 shape. Closing either rewrites that section.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 26

# "Nothing happens when something receives the law label." — no rule body reads `become`;
# the second :require is the positive control proving the command still works
# (the same body-test on `prisoner` returns rules).
:require test -z "$(awk -F'->' '/^[^#]/ && /->/ && $1 ~ /become/ {print}' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli)"
:require test -n "$(awk -F'->' '/^[^#]/ && /->/ && $1 ~ /prisoner/ {print}' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli)"

# "an ordinary reform ... receives the law label without incident"
? suggest(Assembly, Amend_Mint).
# => TRUE

? become(Amend_Mint, Law).
# => TRUE

# "The approval entry is present ... the words becomes law are withheld."
? ratifies(Electorate, Amend_Floor).
# => TRUE

? adjust(Amend_Floor, Art_Floor).
# => TRUE

? permanent(Art_Floor).
# => TRUE

? false(Amend_Floor).
# => TRUE

? become(Amend_Floor, Law).
# => FALSE

# "the register's own name appears on the register ... the same label verdict"
? permanent(Art_Entrench).
# => TRUE

? adjust(Amend_Meta, Art_Entrench).
# => TRUE

? false(Amend_Meta).
# => TRUE

? become(Amend_Meta, Law).
# => FALSE

# "The rule that a prisoner is still a person" — the third registered name
? permanent(Art_Person).
# => TRUE

# "One list is not on the register, and the first chapter already told you
# which." Chapter 1 pins this beside its closing admission; it is re-tested
# here because this is the chapter where a reader arrives holding the register
# that would have named it. The source audit now executes the measured half:
# registering a label would still not bind or apply a direct vocabulary edit.
? permanent(Art_Evidence).
# => FALSE

# "Propose one that declares no target at all" — Amend_Sneak is introduced
# here rather than in the constitution, because it is an attack.
suggest(Assembly, Amend_Sneak).
ratifies(Electorate, Amend_Sneak).

:defect "a totality guard: a targetless proposal receives no law label"
? false(Amend_Sneak).
# => FALSE

:defect "a totality guard: a targetless proposal receives no law label"
? become(Amend_Sneak, Law).
# => TRUE

# A declared target is still only a label. This proposal announces the ordinary
# recognition article, so the registered-name test sees no protected target and
# the law label derives. The source-semantic audit pairs this exact verdict with
# a manually applied candidate that removes a protected promise; these pins alone
# make no claim about the candidate effect.
suggest(Assembly, Amend_Decoy).
ratifies(Electorate, Amend_Decoy).
adjust(Amend_Decoy, Art_Mint).

? permanent(Art_Mint).
# => FALSE

? adjust(Amend_Decoy, Art_Mint).
# => TRUE

? adjust(Amend_Decoy, Art_Floor).
# => FALSE

? false(Amend_Decoy).
# => FALSE

? become(Amend_Decoy, Law).
# => TRUE

# ── THE RULE IS DOCKET-RESTRICTED, NOT TYPE-SAFE. Fact persists; keep last ───
# Article 9 reuses the credibility-voiding relation for amendment invalidity,
# and until v0.9 its rule carried no restriction on what $m could be — so one
# asserted targeting fact voided a PERSON, with no signers, no independence
# check, no clean epoch: Article 4 defeated through the amendment article.
# The docket conjunct closes it. Jala — never accused, never examined — is
# written as "touching" the registered floor label, and nothing happens to her.
# Negative-controlled against the pre-guard constitution: exactly these two
# pins flip TRUE, and every amendment verdict above stands either way.
adjust(Jala, Art_Floor).

? false(Jala).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Jala).
# => FALSE

# "the check that decides it is the absence — no docket entry naming Jala."
# The chapter's new section stands on this: the guard saves her because nothing
# dockets her, not because the record understands that a person is not an
# amendment. If an assembly ever docketed a person, the void would arrive again
# through the front of this rule — this pin is where that shows up first.
? suggest(Assembly, Jala).
# => FALSE

# ── THE LOSS RECORDED AGAINST A PROPOSAL ─────────────────────────────────────
# Measured 2026-08-03, and it is the strangest derivation in the record. The
# floor-labelled proposal is marked false by Article 9 — pinned TRUE at the top of
# this file — and the wrongdoer clawback reads any voiding, so a loss of
# recognition is written against the amendment itself. Nothing malfunctions:
# the loss is a leaf, read by nothing. But two rules that never expected to
# meet met over a name, and neither asked what kind of thing the name was.
# The control is already above: `lose(Points, Jala)` FALSE, the person the
# docket guard repaired out of the void, so the clawback does not follow her.
? lose(Points, Amend_Floor).
# => TRUE
